3 TOP 5 DEEP DIVES

#1 TOOLPORT
Why it matters: this is one of the cleanest current examples of
MONID-like convenience converging with Privately-like enforcement. The
same gateway that reduces tool-context cost also becomes the natural
choke point for credentials, approvals, integrity checks and
per-client capability profiles.
Privately implication: don't build security as an afterthought beside
discovery. Discovery and authority should be one control plane.
Smallest experiment: put two MCPs behind Toolport, give two clients
different profiles, mutate a tool and test whether QP can bind
authorization to the observed tool fingerprint rather than name.
Falsifier: if Toolport cannot expose enough stable identity/version
information to make grants reproducible, it is only an integration
reference.

#2 EXO
Why it matters: Exo pushes RSI beyond prompts/memory/skills into
harness policy itself. It retains only a canonical event log as the
non-mutable historical spine.
Privately implication: self-modifying agents force an explicit answer
to 'what is constitutionally immutable?' QP proofs, authority
receipts, event provenance and the mechanism that decides whether a
mutation may execute probably cannot all be writable by the same
self-improving process.
Smallest experiment: allow Exo to propose changes to a toy policy
module but require an external QP verifier + immutable receipt to
authorize activation.
Falsifier: improvements are mostly cosmetic harness edits with weak
measurable task transfer.

#3 SELF-EVOLVE
Why it matters: explicitly attacks the self-deception problem in
open-ended RSI. LLM proposes; deterministic code adjudicates; changes
live in worktrees; acceptance/rollback isn't decided by the improving
model.
Privately implication: this is close to the correct architecture for
QP processors that are allowed to improve themselves: proposer and
authority/verifier must be separable.
Smallest experiment: run the same repo-improvement task under current
loop vs self-evolve acceptance gate and compare accepted regressions.
Falsifier: the deterministic gate collapses when the objective is
genuinely ambiguous and the fallback judges become equivalent to
self-grading.

#4 AI-VIDEO-SKILL
Why it matters: this is the strongest unseen video neighbour because
it already implements the media version of Dream-RSI: render ->
inspect -> score -> save lesson -> regenerate. It also routes among
multiple current video families rather than coupling to one vendor.
Privately implication: use this as the first autonomous content lab
rather than building another video stack. The useful research question
is whether persisted lessons actually reduce cost/failed generations
across episodes.
Smallest experiment: fixed 30-second Privately explainer, 5
iterations, fixed spend ceiling; measure first-pass acceptance,
regeneration count and cost.
Falsifier: stored lessons do not transfer across shots/topics or cause
prompt overfitting.

#5 AGENT-DISCOVERY-MCP
Why it matters: combines registry discovery and payment in the same
MCP and explicitly exposes a trust gap: metadata can claim x402
support while the real endpoint is dead/unprotected.
Privately implication: capability identity must be empirical. Before a
purchased/discovered service receives authority, probe what it
actually exposes and bind the grant to observed
endpoint/schema/price/effect, not registry metadata.
Smallest experiment: discovery -> preflight endpoint -> compare
advertised vs observed capability -> authorize max $0.01 -> receipt.
Falsifier: ecosystem remains too immature for enough real services to
exercise meaningful routing.

